  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 253 AM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nLow amplitude upper level ridging across the western US will \nmaintain unseasonably warm and dry conditions across the area \nthrough Friday beneath a west-northwest flow aloft. Afternoon \ntemperatures will run slightly lower than those of Monday but still \naround 15 degrees above early October climatological averages. \nAfternoon mixing and a moderate surface pressure gradient across \nthe Rockies will support some breeziness this afternoon, mainly \nacross the plains, with overall lighter winds Wed and Thu as \nsurface high pressure slides east across the Canadian Prairies. \n\nAn upper level trough digging southeast through BC Friday will bring \na significant weather pattern change for the weekend with windy \nconditions developing Friday as southwesterly mid-level flow increases \nahead of the approaching trough. Warm, windy and dry conditions \nFriday will bring elevated fire weather concerns ahead of a cold \nfront Friday night that brings in much cooler and wetter conditions \nfor the weekend. Model guidance is in reasonable agreement to dig \nthe trough southward into the NW US Saturday with typical \ndifferences and spread still at this time range among \nmodels/ensemble members in the eventual evolution of the trough \nthrough the rest of the weekend into early next week. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFire Weather: While wind speeds through Thursday will not be \nsignificant, warm afternoon temperatures and low afternoon \nhumidity will persist with only moderate to locally poor overnight\nhumidly recovery, especially over the central MT mountains and \nadjacent slopes. These condtions may advance the curing and drying\nof lighter (grass/shrub) fuels that are already headed into \nseasonal dormancy. Increased winds with potential for gusts in \nexcess of 40 mph Friday, alongside the continued warmth/dryness \nwill bring an elevated fire weather risk. \n\nWeekend Pattern Change/Impacts: Confidence continues to increase in \nthe overall influx of much cooler temperatures this weekend, along \nwith widespread precipitation and at least some impactful \nmountian snowfall. Temperatures on Saturday are likely to be 25-30\ndegrees colder than Friday with additional cooling arriving \nSaturday night into Sunday. While not unusual for early October, \nthis will be the first of such drastic seasonal swings and will \nfollow an extended period of mild weather. A majority of model \nensemble members support snow levels falling to below 4000 ft by \nSunday, which brings the risk for wet snowfall down to lower \nelevations. However, there is still a very large spread in \nprecipitation amounts/scenarios and timing due to key details \nthat will continue to be resolved over the coming days. Hoenisch",
